{"data":{"attachmentkeynames":["rs"],"desc":"Flow step debugger P3 session engine: debug_step(session, mode, pick) moves a debug session (from debug_start) along its recorded TAPE and returns {status, session, mode, cursor, base, length, event, ready, default, ended, depth, phase, running, hash, tape_hash, branches, ...}. Every executed step is recorded with its inverse (a structural change list {p, o, n}), the persistent-global changes, its view and a canonical state hash; a full snapshot every 64 steps; tape capped (50k steps / 4M nodes, oldest 64-blocks dropped). modes: into = one step; over / out = P2 drive's unit rules; run_to = until the next default pick is pick.target {kind: wire|sync|op, index, frame?, case?}; reexecute = one step, forcing execution (forks); continue = to the end on its OWN THREAD (returns at once with running true; pick.budget, default 10000; pick.wait true joins) - stops at breakpoints, pause, stop, budget, or the end; pause = ask the running owner to stop at its next step boundary; stop = end the session (debug_stop; pick.wait_ms bounds the wait, default 10000); reap = the idle reaper (debug_reap); back (pick.count, default 1) / goto (pick.seq) = roll back or forward without executing, restoring persistent globals (crossed_side_effects lists opaque rust/python command steps passed backwards: rollback restores the interpreter, not the world); edit = set a value at pick.path [\"frames\", i, \"inputs\"|\"results\"|\"out\"|\"args\", ...] to pick.value as an undoable tape step; branch = at a branch's fork point, swap the tape tail with recorded branch pick.id; state = the full view (debug_state). Stepping forward where the tape continues REPLAYS it (replayed counts, nothing executes) while the requested item (pick {kind, index}, or {} = the default) is the one the tape took; another ready item, edit or reexecute FORKS - the old tail is kept as a branch record (max 16). Pick for into/over/out/continue: {} = default, {kind, index} = a READY item (anything else is refused, state unchanged). R4 LIFECYCLE: phase idle | running | pausing | stopping, then terminal (unlinked from globals.flowstep_debug; a tombstone in globals.flowstep_debug_ended, max 64 / 6 h, answers later calls: stop -> ok already:true, state/step -> err phase stopped, ended_by stop|reap). Exactly ONE owner moves the tape: every mutating mode (synchronous call, or the continue worker - the claim moves into its thread) claims idle -> running and is refused while another owner holds it; only the owner leaves running (idle, or the terminal cleanup when stop/reap asked for stopping meanwhile). Each claim has a fresh run token, so a stale owner cannot write last_run, clear running on a newer run, or resurrect an ended session; ids come from a process counter and are never recycled. Pause and stop are COOPERATIVE at step boundaries: the owner checks before EVERY step; an op already executing (rust/python/user code) cannot be preempted - it completes and is recorded exactly once, then the owner stops (latency = the remaining time of that one op). stop waits for the owner's acknowledgement and only then reports stopped/acknowledged true (else acknowledged:false, phase stopping, and the owner ends the session itself after the op). The worker writes a heartbeat (beat) every step, so an active continue is never reaped for lack of HTTP polls; the reaper ends idle sessions after 30 min and only ASKS an owner whose heartbeat is older than 2 h to stop. A static lock guards only these transitions, never a step or user code (scratch.skills.flowstep_r4_check). Robustness: a tape change or alias group that no longer matches the state's shape is REFUSED (status err, or stopped \"refused: ...\" mid-run) with the session's cur, cursor and persistent globals left as they were - never a panic midway; alias links that would put a container inside itself are refused; every state walk (hash, size, diff) is cycle-safe (back-reference marker @cycle) and depth-capped at 256, and alias identity is keyed by (object|array, ref) since ndata's object and array heaps number independently. R3 alias carriage: the interpreter<->persistent-global aliasing at each cursor is recorded on every tape entry (xafter), edit step and snapshot (x), so an execution after goto/replay, at a cap-TRIMMED base, or after an edit relinks the very handles an ordinary run shares (an edit detaches only the edited path). Persistent globals are NOT isolated from other writers - see debug_start's boundary. REPLY EVENT LISTS (R6): `event` is always the tape's step cursor-1 with its seq; `events` lists a move's executed/replayed steps in seq order - into/over/out/run_to/reexecute/continue - and holds at most the FIRST 400 (events_truncated true; goto reaches every step); edit, back, goto and branch replies carry only `event`. Every listed event has a numeric seq equal to its tape index, strictly increasing (scratch.skills.flowstep_r3_seq_check, flowstep_r6_timeline_check). Admin-only; no auth checks inside. R3 D1 alias split/join: a step that re-points a path that was one of several aliases or joins two separate containers is marked tx on its tape entry (txp = example paths); every move crossing a tx entry detaches the restored copy before applying and links the arrival cursor's alias groups (scratch.skills.flowstep_r3_split_check). Persistent keys get the same treatment. Limitation: the canonical state hash ignores everything deeper than 256 levels, so two states that differ only below that depth hash equal - matching hashes there prove nothing; the change lists still carry those values. R3 D2 command bodies: a command frame's body (the callee's SAVED flow, loaded at enter) is stored once per command AND content hash, so a callee re-saved mid-session keeps every frame's OWN body across goto/replay/fork (scratch.skills.flowstep_r3_d2_check). Graph-body contents never enter diff; a changed command body is a new $body ref.","import":"use flowlang::command::Command;\nuse flowlang::datastore::DataStore;\nuse ndata::dataobject::DataObject;\nuse ndata::dataarray::DataArray;\nuse ndata::data::Data;\nuse std::collections::HashMap;\nuse std::panic;","params":[{"name":"session","type":"String"},{"name":"mode","type":"String"},{"name":"pick","type":"JSONObject"}],"returntype":"FLAT","type":"rust"},"id":"wojtzq1a16a46b4bcy9f","readers":[],"time":1793460605715,"username":"system","writers":[]}